body:
I think in some weird condition nvim is trying to draw the screen using the older (bigger) shell size on the new smaller grid size. The original vim code seems to be written to accept this without error (likely one "frame" gets messed up, but gets replaced by a valid redraw just afterwards), but our code is slightly stricter. Maybe we want something like this (equally forgiving as the old vim code) until resize event logic has been refactored:

```diff
diff --git a/src/nvim/screen.c b/src/nvim/screen.c
index fb019a4d2..c6087091b 100644
--- a/src/nvim/screen.c
+++ b/src/nvim/screen.c
@@ -4499,8 +4499,17 @@ static void grid_put_linebuf(ScreenGrid *grid, int row, int coloff, int endcol,
   if (start_dirty == -1) {
     start_dirty = end_dirty;
   }
-  if (clear_end > start_dirty) {
-    ui_line(grid, row, coloff+start_dirty, coloff+end_dirty, coloff+clear_end,
+  
+  // TODO(bfredl): this should not really be necessary. But on some condition
+  // when resizing nvim, a window will be attempted to be drawn on the older
+  // and possibly larger screen size.
+  int draw_start = MIN(coloff+start_dirty, grid->Columns);
+  int draw_end = MIN(coloff+end_dirty, grid->Columns);
+  int draw_clear = MIN(coloff+clear_end, grid->Columns);
+  if (draw_clear > draw_start) {
+    assert(draw_end >= draw_start);
+    assert(draw_clear >= draw_end);
+    ui_line(grid, row, draw_start, draw_end, draw_clear,
             bg_attr, wrap);
   }
 }
```
